    description: "ADR-0023 §6 Phase 2 (Migrate consumers, ~2-3 days estimated by the ADR). Update the consumer-side code that currently filters edges by hardcoded edge-type sets so it queries by (edge_type, dst.kind) instead. This is the consumer-side foundation for the §6 migration: it makes Phase 3's producer-side rename possible without breaking downstream behavior, because Phase 2's queries already accept both the deprecated specialized edge_type AND the canonical type-plus-kind composition.\n\nCONTEXT (for a dev picking this up cold):\n\nADR-0023 (docs/adr/0023-edge-type-relationship-not-endpoints.md, currently Draft) establishes the principle that edge_type should name the *relationship* between src and dst, not properties of the endpoints themselves. Endpoint-shape values (imports_module, query_references, model_reference, type_ref, renders_component, imports_component, plus the bridge / IPC family cgo_bridge, napi_bridge, ffi_bridge, wasm_bridge, etc.) are deprecation candidates whose meaning is captured by dst.kind rather than by the type label.\n\nThe canonical registry (hypergumbo_core.edge_types.EDGE_TYPES, PR #3459) classifies all known emit values on the relationship / endpoint_shape / pending_classification axis. The pre-commit drift linter (PR #3462) and the by-axis view (PR #3463) keep the registry honest. Five further endpoint_shape values were added in PR #3464 to close discrepancies with ADR-0023's deprecation list. ADR-0024 (PR #3468) extracts the four-part axis-declaration template that ADR-0023's instance demonstrates.\n\nPhase 2 takes the next step: have consumers stop relying on deprecated edge_type values directly. They should compose queries from (edge_type == canonical-relationship-name) AND (dst.kind == matching-endpoint-kind) instead. Each consumer migration is reversible — the old edge-type semantics still produce the right answer with the new query shape — so Phase 2 lands before any producer changes (Phase 3).\n\nKNOWN CONSUMERS to migrate (per ADR-0023 §6 Phase 2, transcribed verbatim where the ADR is specific):\n\n1. packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/ranking.py — _IMPORT_EDGE_TYPES (around line 1053) plus the edge-type weight table around line 198. Migrate to (edge_type == 'imports') AND (dst.kind in {'module', 'file'}) or similar — preserving import-edge weighting after Phase 3 collapses imports_module into imports.\n\n2. packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/slice.py — line 640 exclude_imports filter, plus possibly other edge-type filters elsewhere; audit each.\n\n3. packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/sketch.py — any edge-type-based section selection in the sketch budget allocator.\n\n4. packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/io_boundary.py and taint.py — _TRACEABLE_EDGE_TYPES and TAINT_CALL_EDGE_TYPES already use frozenset literals; need an audit to confirm each entry is canonical-relationship-shaped and reclassify any endpoint-shape entries by adding (dst.kind) filters.\n\n5. SCIP merger (packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/scip/) — different shape: switch from 'type-string match' to 'merge by (src, dst, line) and consolidate meta'. Per ADR-0023 §1 finding #3 (the downstream specialization pass mentioned in scip/calls.py:48 does not exist), this is where SCIP-vs-native edge reconciliation belongs.\n\nDEPENDENCIES:\n- Soft dependency on ADR-0023 advancing from Draft to at least Proposed (so the §6 specifics are settled before consumer code is touched).\n- Per-family audits for dispatch/publish family values (currently sitting at pending_classification in the registry) inform what becomes 'canonical' for those families. Phase 2 work on dispatch-family-touching consumers should wait for those audits.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n- Each named consumer is updated to query (edge_type, dst.kind) where the original used a deprecated edge-type set, OR the migration of that consumer is explicitly documented as deferred in this item's discussion thread (with the gating reason — e.g., 'waiting for dispatch-family audit').\n- The drift property test (test_edge_types::test_every_edge_type_named_set_is_a_subset_of_registry) continues to pass after each consumer migration.\n- The merging PR(s) should be tagged awaits_bakeoff_validation per the project's bakeoff-validation discipline (AGENTS.md §Bakeoff Validation Discipline) so a later DEEP cycle confirms no regressions in slice / ranking / sketch output.\n\nSIBLINGS (separate items, not bundled here):\n- ADR-0023 §6 Phase 3 (Unify producers) — analyzer-side rename, depends on Phase 2 landing first.\n- ADR-0023 §6 Phase 4 (Schema bump + deprecation removal) — depends on Phase 3.\n- ADR-0023 advancement from Draft to Proposed/Accepted — separate item.\n- Per-family audits for dispatch family (dispatches_to, routes_to, delegates_to, message_dispatch, annotated_dispatches) and publish family (event_publishes, event_subscribes, crdt_publishes, annotated_publishes) — separate items.\n- find_axis_drift scope broadening (currently only scans packages/) — separate item; affects what registry-completeness Phase 2 starts from but is not strictly blocking.\n- WI-tavas-voror-rakiv-litog-gatad-zonoz-holah-tugil (triage 45 emitted-but-unregistered edge_type values) — depends on Phase 2/3/4 outcomes for its classifications.\n\nBACKGROUND: surfaced as loose-end #3 in the audit-lifecycle session (Apr 2026) — ADR-0023's migration plan was named in the ADR text but the implementation never started; tracker captures the work so it doesn't slip."  # 6494
